1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,479
This is the part that decides which model answers your question.

2
00:00:04,479 --> 00:00:06,967
And it works in three cheap steps.

3
00:00:06,967 --> 00:00:10,948
Step zero is the obvious stuff. Did you attach an image?

4
00:00:10,948 --> 00:00:14,431
Then it must be the vision model. No thinking required.

5
00:00:14,431 --> 00:00:16,919
Step one is a tiny classifier.

6
00:00:16,919 --> 00:00:21,896
This is a small, fast program that reads your question and sorts it into a category.

7
00:00:21,896 --> 00:00:26,374
Is this code, is this a summary, is this a calculation?

8
00:00:26,374 --> 00:00:30,853
It runs on the processor in about 20 milliseconds, faster than you can blink.

9
00:00:30,853 --> 00:00:35,332
Step two is the scorer. It takes the shortlist and checks reality.

10
00:00:35,332 --> 00:00:39,810
Does this model support tools? Is your document going to fit in its memory?

11
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:43,791
Is it loaded right now, or would we have to wake it up?

12
00:00:43,791 --> 00:00:48,270
And then, crucially, it shows you the answer. Every reply carries a small chip.

13
00:00:48,270 --> 00:00:53,744
Click it, and you see the candidates, the scores, and one line explaining why the winner won.

14
00:00:53,744 --> 00:00:58,720
That single panel is what turns a claim into a demonstration.
